  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Warm and humid weather continues across the region, \nwith chances of showers and thunderstorms through Friday. A few \nthunderstorms may produce locally heavy rain. \n\nRegional radar shows a few showers developing across Niagara\ncounty, with heavier rain outside of the forecast area this \nafternoon. Heat index values will top out in the mid to upper \n80s today. \n\nA warm, moist airmass resides across the region this afternoon. \nDewpoints average in the upper 60s across far western NY to the low \n70s east of Rochester. A weak frontal boundary, which caused\nscattered showers earlier this afternoon, has moved east of the\nforecast. Mid- level dry air resides across western NY, where \nshowers have been slow to develop. Isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are expected along and inland from lake breezes, \nmainly across the western Southern Tier and Niagara county this \nafternoon. \n\nMostly clear to partly cloudy skies are expected across the region \ntonight. Valley fog is expected to develop across the western \nSouthern Tier to the Finger Lakes region. \n\nA shortwave trough will approach the region overnight. Increasing \nmoisture and ascent will support scattered showers across far \nwestern NY Friday morning. Diurnally driven convection may be \nsubdued across far western NY, however additional showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to develop from the Genesee Valley to the \nFinger Lakes region. Any thunderstorms will be capable of\nlocalized heavy rainfall. Mostly dry weather is expected across\nthe Saint Lawrence Valley. Cloud cover is expected to keep high\ntemperatures a few degrees lower than previous days.\n\nKey Message 2...Widespread showers and thunderstorms Saturday before \ndrier and less humid airmass briefly settles over our region Sunday.\n\nBroad flow around a Bermuda surface high will carry Atlantic and \nGulf moisture from the Southeast to the Lower Great Lakes for \nSaturday, maintaining the humid airmass for our region. PWats are \nnot out of the norm for early August, with values around 1.5 inches, \nand this moisture combined with MUCape values around 1K - 2K will \nresult in a fair amount of showers and thunderstorms for Saturday.\n\nA weak surface trough will pass across our region Saturday \nafternoon, adding lift for storms already blossoming in the peak \nheating of the afternoon. Severe threat is low, with a deepening \nwarm cloud depth to over 12K feet and 0-6 km bulk shear vectors \ngenerally in the 20 knot range. But rather the risk for heavy rain \nand localized drenching downpours would be the bigger risk. \n\nStorms linger into the evening hours before a weak trough, and drier \nairmass follows later in the night. This drier airmass will bring \nless humidity Sunday to our region, though afternoon high \ntemperatures will remain similar.  \n\nKey Message 3...\n\nUnsettled weather remains for the Lower Great Lakes to start the new \nwork week.\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection (Monday night into Tuesday), \nthough exact timing of subtle shortwave troughs is with still great \nuncertainty at this point in time. As the cooler and drier upper \nlevel low settles over the eastern Great Lakes region chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will begin to diminish once the drier \nairmass becomes established.",
